WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
The first film showed a cascade multiplying one input, then controlling its spread

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.100
and shutoff. What can make an output fade, commit, or reset while input remains?

3
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.780
One answer lies in how output loops back.

4
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:21.653
Feedback begins when output changes its own production.

5
00:00:22.873 --> 00:00:26.573
A return arrow that reduces production is negative feedback.

6
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:31.453
A return arrow that increases production is positive feedback.

7
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.873
Timing and strength decide the result.

8
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:39.213
The same pathway can therefore produce different dynamics.

9
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:43.153
Connections alone cannot predict the time course.

10
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:47.477
Start with a continuing input and a delayed brake.

11
00:00:49.077 --> 00:00:51.057
Output rises before the brake accumulates.

12
00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:56.777
Then negative feedback lowers pathway activity, even though input remains.

13
00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.507
The brake's delay and strength help shape the pulse.

14
00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:03.927
Connections alone did not predict it.

15
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:06.927
Now turn the return arrow forward.

16
00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:13.007
Output strengthens its own production, so a small rise can recruit more activity.

17
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.420
If the loop is strong and includes a steep step, the circuit can settle into low or

18
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.820
high output. A weaker loop may only sharpen the response.

19
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:30.283
An ultrasensitive response is steep, but steepness alone can reverse when input

20
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:35.603
falls. Sufficient positive feedback can add bistability and memory.

21
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:40.217
Then a temporary input may leave output in the high state.

22
00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:43.797
The memory outlasts the input that set it.

23
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.670
A third circuit receives a continuing input.

24
00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.930
Three output traces appear on screen.

25
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.970
Which trace represents adaptation?

26
00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.630
Look for the defining pattern before choosing.

27
00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:07.303
Adaptation is the return toward rest while stimulus remains.

28
00:02:08.763 --> 00:02:11.343
So choose the trace returning toward its starting line.

29
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:14.843
A delayed brake can make a similar shape.

30
00:02:16.203 --> 00:02:18.803
Integral feedback is a special negative feedback.

31
00:02:20.063 --> 00:02:24.023
It keeps correcting the gap from a reference output until the gap is zero.

32
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:31.357
In bacterial chemotaxis, receptor methylation acts as a running total of that gap in

33
00:02:31.357 --> 00:02:32.537
an idealized model.

34
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:36.777
Correction continues until the error reaches zero.

35
00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.037
Engineers call this integral feedback.

36
00:02:40.877 --> 00:02:44.517
CheR adds methyl groups, while CheB removes them.

37
00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:50.867
Changing enzyme amounts can change how quickly correction proceeds and can shift

38
00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:52.147
other steady behaviour.

39
00:02:53.427 --> 00:02:56.167
Yet adaptation precision can remain robust.

40
00:02:57.627 --> 00:03:02.067
Robustness protects a specified property, not every feature of the response.

41
00:03:03.217 --> 00:03:05.517
Place the three designs side by side.

42
00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:09.357
Delayed negative feedback can limit duration.

43
00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:14.857
Sufficient positive feedback can sharpen commitment and preserve memory.

44
00:03:16.437 --> 00:03:18.977
Integral feedback corrects until the error is zero.

45
00:03:20.177 --> 00:03:23.397
It can restore a reference output despite changing parts.

46
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.750
The pathway's arrows begin the explanation, but kinetics finish it.

47
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.590
Loop sign, strength, delay, and component lifetime determine whether output fades,

48
00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:36.430
commits, or adapts.

49
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.450
Feedback reshapes time and reversibility.
